``` - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='adc56a9f-2db0-4a77-851c-9dae211db5c0'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nBlueprint Hotel Reservation\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nA blueprint-compiled version of the hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nblueprint-hotel-reservation\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='daf09b6a-d02d-4242-a03d-2b3f9e1b54d3'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='41540890-c24e-493a-8473-b1a515af155b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. The goal is to identify if there are any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace blueprint-hotel-reservation. The get_services tool from Grafana can list all service names, which is a good starting point. Without knowing the services, I can't proceed to check their health or dependencies.\n\nSo, I'll use get_services to retrieve the list of services. Once I have the services, I can then check each one for metrics, traces, or logs. But first, the list of services is essential. Let me call get_services now.\n</think>\n\nget_services\n\nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the cluster. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a clear overview of the microservices involved. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 230, 'prompt_tokens': 1510, 'total_tokens': 1740, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-8c1d-7c40-93d6-1e6412b020d7-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1510, 'output_tokens': 230, 'total_tokens': 1740}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a4a22004-56b7-4e13-b378-85995e3a5a4f'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user wants me to generate a tool call for the get_services function. The previous step was to choose get_services, so now I need to call that tool. The get_services tool doesn\'t require any arguments, as per the description. So the tool call should just be the name of the tool with empty arguments. Let me make sure I\'m not missing anything. The function\'s parameters are an empty object, so the arguments JSON should be {}.\n\nWait, the user\'s instructions say that for each function call, I need to return a JSON object with the function name and arguments within the tool_call tags. Since get_services has no parameters, the arguments are an empty object. So the correct tool call is {"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}. That should retrieve the list of service names from Grafana. I think that\'s all. No other parameters needed here. Let me double-check the tool\'s description. Yes, the Args section is empty, so no arguments are required. Alright, that\'s the correct call.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-abe1324b780dbb86', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 237, 'prompt_tokens': 2481, 'total_tokens': 2718, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-97a2-70e1-8002-39355ce53429-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-abe1324b780dbb86', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2481, 'output_tokens': 237, 'total_tokens': 2718}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'frontend\', \'product-catalog\', \'product-reviews\', \'checkout\', \'recommendation\', \'quote\', \'ad\', \'load-generator\', \'email\', \'fraud-detection\', \'payment\', \'shipping\', \'image-provider\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'currency\', \'accounting\', \'jaeger-all-in-one\', \'cart\', \'flagd\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='6afc9c74-320c-4c69-9901-bb9eb53e8c3d', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-abe1324b780dbb86'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='651f24a0-9675-4fff-a713-e92babbea8bd'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using get_services, which returned a bunch of service names. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty.\n\nSince there are so many services, I need a way to identify which ones are problematic. The get_traces tool can help here. Traces can show if there are any errors or delays in service interactions. By checking traces for each service, I can spot any anomalies. But since there are multiple services, maybe starting with a broad approach like the dependency graph would give an overview of how services interact, making it easier to spot where issues might be occurring. Alternatively, checking metrics for each service could reveal performance issues or errors. However, the get_dependency_graph tool might be more efficient to visualize the relationships and see if any services are causing cascading issues. Let me go with get_dependency_graph first to understand the architecture and identify any obvious points of failure.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph\n\nTo understand the interactions between microservices and identify potential fault points, I need to visualize the service dependency graph. This will show how services communicate and highlight any abnormal patterns or dependencies that could indicate faults. The graph will help pinpoint services that might be causing or experiencing issues due to their connections.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 281, 'prompt_tokens': 1743, 'total_tokens': 2024, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-a3db-7ed3-84b4-064d5f2fa2e3-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1743, 'output_tokens': 281, 'total_tokens': 2024}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='80154e77-f850-47ac-b479-c633bd17502b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in a microservice-based application called Blueprint Hotel Reservation. They've already used the get_services tool to list all the services, which gave a list like frontend, product-catalog, etc. Now, they want to understand the dependencies between these services.\n\nThe next step they took was using get_dependency_graph. The tool's description says it fetches the service dependency graph from Jaeger's native dependencies API. The argument required is last_n_minutes, which is the time range to look back. Since the user hasn't specified a particular time, but the previous step didn't mention any time frame, I need to decide on a reasonable default. \n\nThe user's goal is to find faults, so looking back a short time, like 15 minutes, makes sense. That way, they can check recent dependencies without overwhelming data. The response from the tool will be a JSON object representing the dependency graph. \n\nBy getting the dependency graph, the user can visualize how services interact. If there's a fault, maybe a service is not communicating properly with others, or there's a bottleneck. For example, if the payment service has a high dependency on the fraud-detection service, and there's a spike in errors there, that could be a root cause. \n\nSo, the tool call should set last_n_minutes to 15. That's a common choice for recent data without being too broad. The user can then analyze the graph for any anomalies, like missing links, high latency, or error-prone connections. This step is crucial before diving into specific metrics or traces.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"last_n_minutes": 15}', 'name': 'get_dependency_graph'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-be6b1e31a15de8d1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 358, 'prompt_tokens': 2710, 'total_tokens': 3068, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-b0b3-7c40-9833-8c0d6c58c700-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_dependency_graph', 'args': {'last_n_minutes': 15}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-be6b1e31a15de8d1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2710, 'output_tokens': 358, 'total_tokens': 3068}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="{\'data\': [], \'total\': 0, \'limit\': 0, \'offset\': 0, \'errors\': None}", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='e9c6fd32-48bf-450a-bb16-a6327f7c5f21', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-be6b1e31a15de8d1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3364fefc-0113-4400-856e-12b88f49f6be'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the dependency graph tool didn\'t return any data. Maybe there\'s an issue with the Jaeger integration or the time frame I chose. Let me try a different approach. Instead of relying on the dependency graph, I should check the actual running pods and deployments to get a clear picture of the services\' current state. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with "kubectl get all" will give me a comprehensive list of pods, deployments, services, and other resources in the namespace. This will help identify if any services are not running or have replicas failing. Let\'s proceed with that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\nTo diagnose the application, I need to inspect the current state of pods, deployments, and services in the namespace. Using `kubectl get all` will provide a comprehensive overview of running workloads, their statuses, and any potential issues like failed pods or misconfigured deployments. This is critical for identifying faulty components directly.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 196, 'prompt_tokens': 1933, 'total_tokens': 2129, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-c251-74d0-b42c-9e6066145447-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1933, 'output_tokens': 196, 'total_tokens': 2129}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='055b2238-3f27-4d02-8f5a-e57642bd041c'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. They've already tried getting services from Grafana, the dependency graph from Jaeger, and now they want to check the Kubernetes resources directly.\n\nThe next step is to use `kubectl get all` in the specified namespace, `blueprint-hotel-reservation`. This command will list all resources like pods, deployments, services, etc. It's essential to check if any pods are in a failed state, deployments have issues, or services are not properly configured. \n\nSince the previous tools didn't return useful data (like the dependency graph came back empty), the kubectl command might provide more concrete information. The user needs to verify the current state of the cluster's workloads to spot any obvious problems. So, the tool call should be `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with the command `kubectl get all -n blueprint-hotel-reservation`.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get all -n blueprint-hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b95e51cc541f27b2', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 229, 'prompt_tokens': 2908, 'total_tokens': 3137, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-cbd4-7cf0-b778-120dc23bfee2-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get all -n blueprint-hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b95e51cc541f27b2', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2908, 'output_tokens': 229, 'total_tokens': 3137}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (Forbidden): replicationcontrollers is forbidden: User "system:serviceaccount:sregym:mcp-server" cannot list resource "replicationcontrollers" in API group "" in the namespace "blueprint-hotel-reservation"\n', id='52469b10-17d8-4d2a-9396-fc5dc46f225e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b95e51cc541f27b2'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='422e9349-10ab-4e49-8bc6-d028be46ba82'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user tried to run "kubectl get all" but got a forbidden error because they can\'t list replicationcontrollers. Hmm, maybe the namespace is restricted, or the service account doesn\'t have enough permissions. Since the tool doesn\'t support interactive commands, I need another approach.\n\nLet me think. The goal is to check the pods and deployments. The user mentioned the namespace is blueprint-hotel-reservation. Maybe I should try getting pods and deployments separately. But the kubectl command might still have permission issues. Wait, the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. Let me check the allowed commands again. The available commands include "kubectl get", so perhaps I can specify just pods and deployments instead of "all".\n\nSo, instead of "kubectl get all", I can run "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" to avoid the forbidden resource. That way, I can get the necessary info without hitting the replicationcontrollers. Let me try that. If those commands are allowed, it should give the current state of pods and deployments, which are crucial for diagnosing issues. If there\'s an error again, maybe I need to adjust the command further, but this is a safer approach.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\nThe previous attempt to retrieve all resources failed due to permission restrictions on `replicationcontrollers`. To avoid this, I will instead directly query **pods** and **deployments** separately, which are more likely to provide actionable insights without triggering the same error. This approach narrows the scope while still capturing critical workload states. \n\n**Tool call for pods:**\n```json\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation"}}\n```\n\n**Tool call for deployments:**\n```json\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get deployments -n blueprint-hotel-reservation"}}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 403, 'prompt_tokens': 2152, 'total_tokens': 2555, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-dc45-77c3-8030-673f95a89676-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2152, 'output_tokens': 403, 'total_tokens': 2555}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b0142ec2-b4eb-4ebe-ac7e-75f0fc84f19d'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me figure out what to do next. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with multiple microservices. They\'ve already tried getting the list of services, the dependency graph, and then attempted to get all resources using kubectl, but faced a permission error with replicationcontrollers.\n\nSo, the next step is to focus on the pods and deployments without hitting the same error. The user mentioned that the namespace is blueprint-hotel-reservation. Since the previous kubectl get all command failed, they need to break it down into smaller commands that don\'t access restricted resources.\n\nFirst, checking the pods. The command "kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation" should list all pods in that namespace. This will show if any pods are in a failed state, crashing, or not running. Then, checking deployments with "kubectl get deployments -n blueprint-hotel-reservation" will show the status of each deployment, like if they\'re up-to-date, available, or have issues with replicas.\n\nBy running these two commands separately, the user can gather information about the current state of the application\'s workloads without encountering the permission error. If any pods are not running or deployments are not healthy, that could indicate the root cause of the problem. For example, if a pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, there might be an application error or configuration issue. If a deployment has zero available replicas, there could be a problem with the deployment configuration or resource limits.\n\nAfter getting the pod and deployment statuses, the next step would be to check the logs of any failing pods using kubectl logs, or look into the metrics and traces to see if there are any performance issues or errors in the service dependencies. But first, confirming the current state of the pods and deployments is essential.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b5190846670f5663', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 399, 'prompt_tokens': 3211, 'total_tokens': 3610, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb93-eeb4-7792-96e3-678e7e59c585-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b5190846670f5663', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3211, 'output_tokens': 399, 'total_tokens': 3610}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nfrontend-service-5449c956d4-lhpsh   1/1     Running   0          10m\ngeo-db-7685474796-4qlcd             1/1     Running   0          10m\ngeo-service-7f765f4749-kqlp4        1/1     Running   0          10m\njaeger-69f774d94f-9xf52             1/1     Running   0          10m\nprofile-cache-5df456bcd5-6n8zv      1/1     Running   0          10m\nprofile-db-f5f46658f-c2tdb          1/1     Running   0          10m\nprofile-servic