y can run kubectl get deployments to see if any deployments are not in a healthy state. Also, checking the replicas and availability of each deployment could help. If a deployment has zero replicas or is in a pending state, that\'s a problem. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they should check the services to see if any are not accessible. But the user hasn\'t checked services yet. However, since the initial get_services from Grafana gave a list of services, maybe they should verify if those services are properly exposed. But the user\'s next step should be to check deployments because the pods are all running, but deployments manage the replicas. If a deployment is not scaling correctly, that could cause issues. So the next tool call should be to get deployments using kubectl. That makes sense. Let me confirm the available commands. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run "kubectl get deployments -n blueprint-hotel-reservation". That\'s the next logical step.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\nThe pods are all running, but I need to verify the health of deployments to ensure they are properly managing replicas and not experiencing scaling issues or rollout failures. Deployments often handle state transitions, and anomalies here could indicate root causes like misconfigured replica sets or failed updates. I\'ll check deployments next. \n\n**Tool call:**\n```json\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get deployments -n blueprint-hotel-reservation"}}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 470, 'prompt_tokens': 2999, 'total_tokens': 3469, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb94-041f-7f51-a313-249ac98799fa-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'inpu