healthy, then maybe the issue is elsewhere. But if there\'s a problem, like a deployment with zero replicas or pending, that\'s a clue. \n\nSo the correct tool call here is to get the deployments using kubectl. The user needs to check if the deployments are managing their replicas correctly. If there\'s an issue, they can then look into the specific deployment\'s details, logs, or events. But for now, the next step is to retrieve the deployments\' status.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n blueprint-hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a4f1e0e2a498d2ea', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 479, 'prompt_tokens': 4010, 'total_tokens': 4489, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb94-1a10-7492-8d12-10bb2857a722-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n blueprint-hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a4f1e0e2a498d2ea', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4010, 'output_tokens': 479, 'total_tokens': 4489}), ToolMessage(content='NAME               READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\nfrontend-service   1/1     1            1           11m\ngeo-db             1/1     1            1           11m\ngeo-service        1/1     1            1           11m\njaeger             1/1     1            1           11m\nprofile-cache      1/1     1            1           11m\nprofile-db         1/1     1            1           11m\nprofile-service    1/1     1            1           11m\nrate-cache         1/1     1            1           11m\nrate-db            1/1     1            1           11m\nrate-service       1/1     1            1           11m\nrecomd-db          1/1     1            1           11m\nrecomd-service     1/1     1            1           11m\nreserv-cache       1/1     1            1           11m\nreserv-db          1/1     1            1           11m\nreserv-service     1/1     1            1           11m\nsearch-service     1/1     1            1           11m\nuser-db            1/1     1            1           11m\nuser-service       1/1     1            1           11m\n', id='1643a4ae-b4c6-41dc-a4d3-b1ca1192c349', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a4f1e0e2a498d2ea'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='47c9c501-bd50-4212-a18d-952af957ad68'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in the Blueprint Hotel Reservation application. So far, they\'ve checked the services using get_services, the dependency graph with get_dependency_graph, and the pods and deployments with kubectl commands. All the pods and deployments are showing as healthy with READY, UP-TO-DATE, and AVAILABLE set to 1. The dependency graph returned an empty data array, which might mean there\'s no data or an issue with the tool.\n\nSince the initial checks didn\'t show any obvious issues, the next step is to look deeper. The user might need to check the metrics or logs for any anomalies. The get_metrics tool can query Prometheus for real-time data. Maybe there are metrics like error rates, latency, or resource usage that are abnormal. For example, if a service has high CPU or memory usage, or if there are a lot of errors, that could indicate a problem.\n\nAlternatively, checking the logs with kubectl logs could reveal specific errors or exceptions. But the user already tried kubectl get all, so maybe they need to look into specific services. However, the available tools include get_metrics, which is a good next step. They can use PromQL to check for errors or other issues. For instance, querying for the number of HTTP 500 errors or latency percentiles. If there are no errors in the metrics, maybe the issue is with the application logic or external dependencies. But since the dependency graph didn\'t show anything, perhaps the problem is in the application\'s configuration or environment variables. However, without more data, it\'s hard to tell. The user should use get_metrics to gather more information.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics\n\nThe pods and deployments appear healthy, but this doesn\'t rule out subtle issues like high latency, resource bottlenecks, or silent failures (e.g., 500 errors). To investigate deeper, I\'ll query real-time metrics (e.g., error rates, latency, or resource usage) using Prometheus. This will help identify anomalies in service behavior that aren\'t visible through pod status alone. \n\n**Tool call:**\n```json\n{"name": "get_metrics", "arguments": {"query": "sum(rate(http_requests_total{status!~\\"2[0-9][0-9]\\"}[$__interval])) by (service)"}}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 482, 'prompt_tokens': 3560, 'total_tokens': 4042, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb94-3369-75d0-bd0b-4af9d0b447d8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3560, 'output_tokens': 482, 'total_tokens': 4042}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0dc8c5c7-4560-417a-bf0d-4ab0298c9066'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the Blueprint Hotel Reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems to be running normally. But since the problem might not be obvious from just the pod statuses, they need to look deeper.\n\nThe next step was to use the get_metrics tool with a query for HTTP error rates. The query they used is sum(rate(http_requests_total{status!~"2[0-9][0-9]"}[$__interval])) by (service). This should give them the rate of non-2xx HTTP responses per service, which would indicate any services that are returning errors. \n\nBut wait, the user hasn\'t provided the actual results of this query yet. They just made the tool call. So in the next step, they need to process the response from get_metrics. If the metrics show any services with a high error rate, that could point to the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, if there are no errors, maybe they should check other metrics like latency or resource usage (CPU, memory). For example, if a service has high latency, it might be causing issues downstream. Or if a service is using too much CPU, it could be a bottleneck.\n\nAnother thing to consider is the dependency graph. Earlier, the get_dependency_graph returned an empty data array, which might mean there are no dependencies recorded, or the tool isn\'t capturing them. But since the user already checked pods and deployments, maybe the issue is in the services\' configurations or network policies.\n\nWait, the user also has the get_traces tool. If there are errors in the metrics, using get_traces could help identify specific issues in the service\'s operations. For example, if the recommendation service has a high error rate, tracing could show which specific calls are failing.\n\nBut first, the current step is to generate the tool call for get_metrics. The query they used is correct for checking HTTP errors. Once they get the results, they can analyze which services have non-zero error rates. If there are errors, they can then use get_traces on those services to find the root cause. If there are no errors, they might need to look into other metrics like latency or resource usage.\n\nSo the next step after the get_metrics call is to wait for the response. If the response shows any issues, proceed with tracing or other checks. If not, maybe check the logs or other metrics. But since the user is in the thinking stage now, they need to decide which tool to call next based on the previous step\'s outcome. However, since the tool call for get_metrics hasn\'t been executed yet, the user is preparing to make that call. \n\nWait, in the previous message, the user made the get_metrics tool call. Now, the system is waiting for the response. The user needs to generate the tool call, which they did. Now, the next step would be to process the response. But since the user hasn\'t received the actual data yet, they might need to wait for the tool\'s response. However, in the context of this exercise, the user is simulating the process. So after making the get_metrics call, the next step would be to analyze the returned data. If there are errors, proceed with tracing. If not, maybe check other metrics or logs.\n\nBut in the current setup, the user is supposed to generate the tool call, and then the system provides the response. So the user\'s next action is to generate the get_metrics tool call, which they did. Now, the system would return the metrics data. Based on that data, the user would decide the next steps. For example, if the metrics show high error rates in the recommendation service, they would use get_traces on that service. If there are no errors but high latency, they might check the network policies or resource limits.\n\nIn this case, the user\'s last tool call was get_metrics, and the response hasn\'t been provided yet. So the next step is to wait for the metrics data. But since the user is in the thinking stage, they need to decide what to do next. However, the user has already made the get_metrics call, so the next step would be to process the response. But since the response isn\'t available here, the user might need to proceed with another tool if the metrics don\'t show anything. Alternatively, they might use get_traces on specific services if they suspect issues based on other information.\n\nBut given the current information, the user should wait for the get_metrics response. If the metrics show no issues, they might need to check other aspects like logs or configurations. However, since the user is limited to the tools provided, they might need to use get_traces on specific services if they have a hypothesis. But without the metrics data, it\'s hard to say. So the next logical step is to process the get_metrics response and then decide accordingly.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum(rate(http_requests_total{status!~\\"2[0-9][0-9]\\"}[$__interval])) by (service)"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b1150f7eb33dd121', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1067, 'prompt_tokens': 4603, 'total_tokens': 5670, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb94-49eb-7b61-8a0f-9697ac56e565-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum(rate(http_requests_total{status!~"2[0-9][0-9]"}[$__interval])) by (service)'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b1150f7eb33dd121', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4603, 'output_tokens': 1067, 'total_tokens': 5670}), ToolMessage(content="('[prom_mcp] Error querying get_metrics: 400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: http://prometheus-server.observe.svc.cluster.local:80/api/v1/query?query=sum%28rate%28http_requests_total%7Bstatus%21~%222%5B0-9%5D%5B0-9%5D%22%7D%5B%24__interval%5D%29%29+by+%28service%29', 101)", id='1ed60159-049f-4a49-8d4d-6006195a97fe', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b1150f7eb33dd121'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5283f249-71d2-4fa8-8623-f7097df4b64e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user is trying to diagnose issues in the Blueprint Hotel Reservation application. So far, they\'ve checked the services, deployments, and pods, and everything seems to be running normally. The next step was to check metrics, but the query for HTTP errors failed. The error message indicates a Bad Request, which probably means the Prometheus query is incorrect.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the query isn\'t working. The original query was trying to sum the rate of HTTP requests with non-2xx status codes. But maybe the metric name or labels are different in their setup. Common metrics in Prometheus might be named differently, like \'http_requests_total\' but with different labels or a different structure.\n\nSince the user doesn\'t have access to the exact metric names or labels, maybe they should start by listing the available metrics related to the services. Using the \'list\' endpoint in Prometheus could help identify the correct metric names. Alternatively, checking the metrics for each service individually might reveal if there are any errors or anomalies.\n\nAnother approach is to simplify the query. Instead of using a complex rate calculation, they could check for the count of error status codes directly. For example, using \'count by (service) (http_requests_total{status!~"2[0-9][0-9]"})\' might be more straightforward. But without knowing the exact metric name, this is speculative.\n\nThe user might also need to verify if the Prometheus server is correctly configured and if the services are exposing the necessary metrics. If the metrics aren\'t available, the query will fail. Alternatively, checking the logs of the services or using the Jaeger traces could provide more insights into any issues that aren\'t captured by metrics.\n\nSince the previous metric query failed, the next logical step is to check the available metrics to ensure the correct names and labels are used. If that\'s not possible, maybe looking into the logs or traces for specific errors could help identify the root cause without relying on the metrics query.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics\n\nThe failed query suggests either incorrect metric names/labels or a misconfigured Prometheus setup. To troubleshoot, I\'ll first check **basic metric availability** for the services by querying a simpler metric (e.g., pod CPU/memory usage) to verify if Prometheus is correctly scraping metrics. This will help isolate whether the issue is with the query itself or the metric exposure.\n\n**Tool call:**\n```json\n{"name": "get_metrics", "arguments": {"query": "sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{pod!~\\"jaeger-.*\\"})"}}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 529, 'prompt_tokens': 3926, 'total_tokens': 4455, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb94-8173-7942-930c-862603617010-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3926, 'output_tokens': 529, 'total_tokens': 4455}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a7ee16bf-2add-4786-a3ef-1b1302422f42'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their microservices application. They've already checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems to be running fine. But there's a problem they need to find.\n\nEarlier, they tried to get metrics using a query for HTTP errors, but it failed with a 400 Bad Request. The error might be because the metric name or labels are incorrect. The user is using Prometheus, so maybe the metrics aren't exposed properly or the query syntax is wrong.\n\nNow, the assistant is suggesting a simpler query to check container CPU usage, excluding the jaeger pods. This makes sense because if the basic metrics are available, it would indicate that Prometheus is correctly set up and scraping the metrics. If this query works, the issue is probably with the original HTTP error query. If it fails, then maybe the problem is with the metric exposure or Prometheus configuration.\n\nThe user's next step is to run this new query. If it returns data, they can then try the original query again. If not, they might need to check the Prometheus logs or verify that the services are exposing the necessary metrics. This approach is methodical, starting simple and moving to more complex queries, which is a good way to troubleshoot.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{pod!~\\"jaeger-.*\\"})"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8609fe6563d10ed0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 302, 'prompt_tokens': 4953, 'total_tokens': 5255, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb94-9a1e-7731-b58d-5893fe4d9188-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{pod!~"jaeger-.*"})'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8609fe6563d10ed0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4953, 'output_tokens': 302, 'total_tokens': 5255}), ToolMessage(content='