vice. They can check the deployment or service YAML files to see what labels are applied. Alternatively, they can use a different label or selector to find the right pods. If the payment service isn't properly labeled, the logs won't be retrieved, leading to dead ends.\n\nAnother possibility is that the payment service is a StatefulSet or DaemonSet, which might have different naming conventions. The user should check the type of workload managing the payment service. If it's a Deployment, the pods would have the deployment's name as part of their name. If it's a StatefulSet, the pod names would follow a specific pattern.\n\nSince the user is stuck, the best approach is to first confirm the correct label or pod name for the payment service. If that's not possible, they might need to check other services involved in the reservation process, like 'checkout' or 'user-service', to see if those have logs that could indicate issues. Alternatively, they could look into the metrics again with the correct query to see if there's any error rate or latency spikes that point to a specific service.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs payment-7df54684d4-2xgkl -n blueprint-hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-816179e6886493ca', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 647, 'prompt_tokens': 9484, 'total_tokens': 10131, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb95-7f02-7de1-a173-b92734c8ae0e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs payment-7df54684d4-2xgkl -n blueprint-hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-816179e6886493ca', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9484, 'output_tokens': 647, 'total_tokens': 10131}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nerror: error from server (NotFound): pods "payment-7df54684d4-2xgkl" not found in namespace "blueprint-hotel-reservation"\n', id='59bb2dca-7884-419f-8312-88795703bb00', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-816179e6886493ca'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3a9d8d43-c267-471d-ab39-ebcd85328a84'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to get the logs for the payment service but got a "Not Found" error. The previous attempt used a specific pod name that doesn\'t exist. Maybe the pod name in the logs command was incorrect.\n\nFirst, I should check the list of payment pods again to get the correct name. The user tried using "payment-7df54684d4-2xgkl", but that\'s not present. So, I need to run the command to list all payment pods in the namespace to find the right one.\n\nThe correct approach is to first get the list of pods with the label app=payment. Then, use the actual pod name from that list in the logs command. Since the user can\'t use command substitution, they have to manually input the pod name. But since the user might not know the exact name, the next step is to retrieve the list again to ensure the correct pod name is used. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the pods again with the app=payment label to find the correct pod name. Once that\'s done, the logs can be retrieved using the correct pod name. This will resolve the "Not Found" error.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\nThe pod name `payment-7df54684d4-2xgkl` was not found, so I need to re-verify the list of payment pods to ensure the correct pod name is used. This will prevent future "Not Found" errors and ensure logs are retrieved from an active pod. \n\n**Tool call:**\n```json\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation -l app=payment"}}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 372, 'prompt_tokens': 8735, 'total_tokens': 9107, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb95-a184-7902-ba20-7f91320c5bd7-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8735, 'output_tokens': 372, 'total_tokens': 9107}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0e447587-3c1d-449a-8446-50c4d272db70'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the Blueprint Hotel Reservation application, which consists of many microservices. They\'ve already checked the pods and deployments, and everything seems to be running normally. But there\'s a problem somewhere, and they need to find the root cause.\n\nFirst, they tried using get_services to list all services, which gave a list including \'payment\'. Then they checked the dependency graph but got an empty response. Next, they checked pods and deployments, which were all healthy. Then they tried querying metrics but faced issues with the query syntax. After that, they looked into traces for \'reserv-service\' and \'payment\' but got empty results. When trying to get logs for the payment service, they encountered a "Not Found" error because the pod name was incorrect.\n\nSo, the next step is to recheck the list of payment pods to get the correct pod name. The user previously ran \'kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation -l app=payment\' and got a list, but maybe the pod name changed or there was a typo. By running the same command again, they can ensure they have the current list of payment pods. Once they have the correct pod name, they can then retrieve the logs without errors. This will help them check for any runtime issues or errors in the payment service that might be causing the problem.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation -l app=payment"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a95a742b30646301', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 332, 'prompt_tokens': 9756, 'total_tokens': 10088, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb95-b3cf-70a1-8320-bc8847e2b1cc-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n blueprint-hotel-reservation -l app=payment'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a95a742b30646301', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9756, 'output_tokens': 332, 'total_tokens': 10088}), ToolMessage(content='', id='5da0eca8-c7ba-4b19-aebf-640b8dac0956', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a95a742b30646301'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8fcef9d9-0626-450b-8e32-09f62f224530')]]
